{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>That you may pass into the covenant of HaShem your Elohim.</b> The fact that throughout the entire parashah it speaks to them in the plural, whereas in the verse that mentions the passing of the covenant it speaks to them in the singular — this is a decisive proof that the essence of the covenant is for the sake of mutual responsibility (arvut). For from the side of mutual responsibility all Israel are like a single man: just as when one has a wound in some particular limb, his entire body feels it and suffers, so too when one person sins they all feel it, as it says regarding the sin of Achan: ״חָטָא יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Joshua 7:11). And so they said in the Midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 4:6): ״שֶׂה פְזוּרָה יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Jeremiah 50:17) — why were Israel compared to a sheep? Just as a sheep, when one of its limbs is stricken, all of them feel it, so too Israel — Achan alone sinned, yet it is written ״חָטָא יִשְׂרָאֵל.״ And it appears to me that the entire exposition was derived from the word ״פְזוּרָה,״ for it means to say: since they are like a sheep, in that when one of its limbs is stricken all of them feel it — and if so, they are like a single man, that the pain of one limb is felt throughout his entire body — then by rights they should all be bound in a single bond as one man; yet even so they are פְּזוּרִים, scattered, and each man separates from his brother, neither seeking his fellow's welfare nor his good all the days; and a man does not say in his heart: Are not all the evil occurrences that befall my fellow — upon me they turn, and his cup will pass over me as well. Therefore it says here: ״לְעָבְרְךָ בִּבְרִית ה׳״ — in the singular, for through this covenant you will perforce be in a single bond as one man. And on this principle of mutual responsibility all the verses of this parashah will be explained, as their explanation will appear shortly, with the help of the Blessed Name." "<b>You are standing today.</b> In the Midrash (Tanchuma 1): Why is the parashah of ״אַתֶּם נִצָּבִים״ juxtaposed to the curses? Because when Israel heard ninety-eight curses besides the forty-nine in the Torah of the Priests, their faces turned white, etc.; see the entire text in Rashi. And here there is room for a great doubt regarding what comfort Israel received — as it were, empty consolation. For the fact that He did not bring annihilation upon them until now is because their measure was not yet full for all the curses of the reproof to fall upon them. And if you would say that they provoked much anger toward the Omnipresent, blessed be He, and multiplied their guilt until they deserved all those curses, yet HaShem blessed be He in His mercy did not deal with them destruction commensurate with all their sin — if so, then Moses wrote them a writ together with its cancellation, and he warned them of all those reproofs for naught, for they would never fear them, nor would dread of those curses rise upon their heads, for they would say: Behold, we have already sinned thus and thus, and the curse did not alight upon us; and the man Moses promised us that even if we continue to sin, no evil will touch us. And if so, Moses did two contradictory things, for first he forewarned them of the curses so that they would fear them, and afterward he comforted them and spoke to their heart, saying that even if they sin they would not be consumed through the curses. And if you would say that the curses would indeed take effect but would not suffice to destroy them entirely — behold, they were already promised this from the mouth of the Almighty, as it says (Leviticus 26:44): ״לֹא מְאַסְתִּים וְלֹא גְעַלְתִּים לְכַלֹּתָם״ etc. And there is a further difficulty: why were they not panicked by those forty-nine curses in the Torah of the Priests, for in them too there is more than enough to turn any face pale? And one may further examine the wording: why did it say 'a hundred minus two'? If two are missing, it is not a hundred, and it should have said 'ninety-eight,' just as it said 'besides the forty-nine,' etc. And what is the meaning of what it says: 'Thus He gave you light, and is destined to give you light,' etc. — who is this 'destined'? And how do we know that Moses said all these things?'" "And what appears correct to say in this matter is that in all the curses explicitly stated in the Torah of the Priests and in the parashah of Ki Tavo, in all of them there is room for a stiff-necked man to remove the fear of HaShem from before his eyes and to assure himself peace, to seek salvation through some human contrivance — in the manner spoken of the generation of the Flood: ״קַל הוּא עַל פְּנֵי מַיִם״ (Job 24:18) — we have small light boats that can sail upon the face of the waters. And so too regarding these curses — by way of parable, it is written: ״וְהָיוּ שָׁמֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר עַל רֹאשְׁךָ בַּרְזֶל,״ which implies specifically what is above your head and not what is above the heads of the other nations, for there the rain will be; if so, a stiff-necked man may say: I will go and purchase grain from the other nations, for they have food. And likewise with pestilence and sword, he may say: I will flee for my life, as it says (Proverbs 22:5): ״צִנִּים פַּחִים בְּדֶרֶךְ עִקֵּשׁ שׁוֹמֵר נַפְשׁוֹ יִרְחַק מֵהֶם.״ And if so, in all the curses their faces did not turn white, for they said: There is room to be saved from them by human contrivance. But if you exaggerate a man's punishment — telling him that something will be done to him without specifying what his punishment is — then he cannot guard himself against you, for he will not know from what to beware. Now, regarding those curses of the plains of Moav, they are indeed a hundred, for the author of the Midrash counted them and found a hundred; but included among them were two whose nature was not specified — what they are — namely what is said: ״גַּם כָּל חֳלִי וְכָל מַכָּה אֲשֶׁר לֹא כָתוּב בְּסֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֶּה.״ And without doubt this author of the Midrash did not count 'every illness and every plague' within the number ninety-eight, but counted ninety-eight besides them, and together with them there are a hundred; yet of those hundred, two are 'missing' — not truly missing, but missing in that their nature was not specified. If so, it is impossible to guard oneself against them. Therefore they panicked, they trembled, trembling seized them, on account of those two that were missing and whose nature was sealed, and they feared that annihilation would be wrought upon them." "And Moses spoke to their heart: The matter is not as you thought — that the nature of these two was concealed so that you would have no means to guard against them. For behold, many times you provoked anger and these two curses did not fall, even though you deserved that they should. And if you would say: If so, why were the curses stated in the warning, and why were they not specified? — because they speak of the punishment of the World to Come, which is hidden. For by reason of man's being enmeshed in the darkness of his matter, he cannot comprehend the nature of that punishment, just as the Torah concealed the nature of the spiritual reward for this same reason. And the explanation of the matter is that Moses said: I am forewarning you ninety-eight curses, all of them in this world; ״גַּם כָּל חֳלִי וְכָל מַכָּה״ — that which pertains to the sinner in the World to Come according to his wickedness — ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא כָתוּב בְּסֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֶּה,״ for the Torah did not speak of this punishment at all — this too shall fall upon the heads of the wicked. And this is what it says: ״אַתֶּם נִצָּבִים הַיּוֹם״ — like this day, which darkens and brightens, so too you endure in eternal perdurance; and even after the sun and the moon grow dark on the day of death, you shall return to shine in the eternal light of HaShem in the World to Come. And once it is granted that man has existence even after his death — behold, through this you will understand the meaning of the verse ״גַּם כָּל חֳלִי וְכָל מַכָּה אֲשֶׁר לֹא כָתוּב בְּסֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֹּאת.״ And this is what they said: 'Thus He gave you light, and is destined to give you light,' for just as He gives you light in this world, so does the sun shine there also for the future; and the curses and the sufferings sustain you and establish you standing before Me, as they said (Berakhot 5a): Three good gifts did the Holy One, blessed be He, give to Israel, and all of them were given only through suffering, and one of them is the World to Come, as it says (Proverbs 6:23): ״וְדֶרֶךְ חַיִּים תּוֹכְחוֹת מוּסָר.״ And after Moses revealed to them the secret of the World to Come, which previously they had not known, behold, through this their minds were set at ease by understanding those two concealed blows, as stated."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:04.039900783Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}